## 2. Exceptional-face floor

The exact stopped-train ledger is

\[
T=q+2f-k.
\tag{PC109.13}
\]

Since `q<=P_F`, `k>=0`, and `P_F/D->pi` in the full-core Type-II branch,

\[
T-{q\over2}
={q\over2}+2f-k
\le {P_F\over2}+2f.
\]

Combining with (PC109.1),

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {f\over D}
\ge f_{109}:=
\sqrt{\Delta_2}-{\pi\over4}+{g\over2}
=0.167459643466509\ldots .
}
\tag{PC109.14}
\]

Therefore

\[
\liminf {Q\over D}\ge f_{109},
\qquad
\liminf {Q+4f\over D}\ge5f_{109}
=0.837298217332545\ldots .
\tag{PC109.15}
\]

---

## 3. Endpoint/interface floor

Define

\[
J_D:=(b-P_F)+(Q+4f).
\tag{PC109.16}
\]

The train handshake gives

\[
J_D=2T-P_F.
\tag{PC109.17}
\]

Put

\[
C_{109}:=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+g
=1.905715613727914\ldots .
\]

Because

\[
2T=q+2(T-q/2),
\qquad q\ge q-k,
\]

(PC109.1), (PC109.2), and `P_F/D->pi` give

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {J_D\over D}
\ge j_{109}:=t_{109}+2C_{109}-\pi
=2.490867811845480\ldots .
}
\tag{PC109.18}
\]

---

## 4. Exact correlated defect `H_D`

Define

\[
H_D:=(b-P_F)+(Q+2f).
\tag{PC109.19}
\]

The outer ledger is

\[
b+Q=2(q-k).
\tag{PC109.20}
\]

Eliminating `f` between (PC109.16) and (PC109.20) gives the exact identity

\[
\boxed{
H_D={J_D\over2}+(q-k)-{P_F\over2}.
}
\tag{PC109.21}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {H_D\over D}
\ge h_{109}:={j_{109}\over2}+t_{109}-{\pi\over2}
=1.495666817107288\ldots .
}
\tag{PC109.22}
\]

This is stronger than throwing away the `q-k` correlation through the older bound `H_D>=3J_D/5`.

