## 6. Raw chamber slack versus genuine residual

The exact chamber-interface cancellation theorem remains

\[
\Sigma_{ch}=J_D+a+L_{add}+R_F,
\qquad a,L_{add},R_F\ge0.
\tag{PC109.27}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {\Sigma_{ch}\over D}
\ge j_{109}
=2.490867811845480\ldots .
}
\tag{PC109.28}
\]

This remains mandatory boundary credit rather than fixed-wall packing spare.

The true residual-budget theorem gives

\[
R_F+\Sigma_{ext}<{P_K\over2}+P_F-q.
\]

Using `q>=q-k`, Cauchy's perimeter bounds, and (PC109.2),

\[
\boxed{
\limsup {R_F+\Sigma_{ext}\over D}
\le {3\pi\over2}-t_{109}
=2.891359742405245\ldots .
}
\tag{PC109.29}
\]

Adding the existing coarse loaded-rhombus allowance gives the joint-puncture coefficient

\[
3.863371892162530\ldots,
\tag{PC109.30}
\]

still far from contradiction.

