## 7. Low-residual pentagon source objects also improve

The low-`t` pentagon-sea formula has the form

\[
P(t,\delta,\kappa)
=\pi+C_{109}-{5t\over2}+\delta+{\kappa\over2}.
\tag{PC109.31}
\]

At the most permissive profile `t=t_109`, `delta=kappa=0`,

\[
P_{109}=0.494735172369096\ldots .
\tag{PC109.32}
\]

Therefore, on the additional branch `R_F=o(D)`, the existing pentagon extraction gives

\[
\boxed{{V_{flat}\over D}\ge0.098947034473819\ldots-o(1),}
\tag{PC109.33}
\]

\[
\boxed{{H_{tooth}\over D}\ge0.054970574707677\ldots-o(1),}
\tag{PC109.34}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
{C_{flat}\over D},\ {T_{tooth}\over D}
\ge0.038056551720699\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{PC109.35}
\]

The increase is numerically modest because the §171 floor rises with the train gap and partially offsets the direct `C_109` gain. The important new structural inputs remain the loaded degree-two defect payment and the unloaded stress-through theorem.

